+### Accordion classes
+
+The accordion key ships whole: the group and its published properties, the header button in its
+expanded and collapsed states with the chevron each state draws, the hover and focus lifts, the item
+borders and the corners of the boundary items, the body, and the flush group. The component partial
+loads between the card partial and the breadcrumb partial in the components layer, at the barrel's
+Bootstrap order.
+
+Each state is a class set in markup. A button without the `collapsed` class is the expanded one: it
+paints from the active slots, draws a line one border width deep along its bottom edge where it
+meets its panel, and turns its chevron half a revolution. A button carrying the `collapsed` class
+paints from the resting slots. Each panel carries the `accordion-collapse` class beside the
+`collapse` class, so the collapse key hides it until it carries the `show` class. No rule here sets
+or clears a class. The Collapse plugin that moves an item between the states, and whose `parent`
+option closes the other items of a group when one opens, is an engine obligation § Compatibility
+records under the J-ENGINE owner.
+
+Each published property is declared on the `.accordion` class. A declaration on an ancestor is
+outranked there, so the accordion itself is where a consumer retunes one. A color reads the
+compatibility variable Bootstrap itself names, so the item, the resting button, and the expanded
+button retune with the color mode.
+
+The button lays its label and its chevron out as one full-width row, and the chevron's automatic
+start margin pushes it to the row's end. The label's `text-align: left` declaration and that
+`margin-left` declaration are physical, as the release writes them.
+
+The group's outer corners belong to its boundary items. The first item rounds its top corners and
+the last item rounds its bottom corners, and each later item gives up its top border, so
+neighbouring items share one line. The button at each end rounds its own corners to the
+`--bs-accordion-inner-border-radius` value, the outer radius less the border width, so its fill
+stays inside the curve. The last button rounds its bottom corners only while it carries the
+`collapsed` class: an expanded last button meets its panel, and the panel takes the outer radius
+instead.
+
+The `accordion-flush` class runs the items edge to edge. It drops the side borders, the top border
+of the first item, and the bottom border of the last item, and it squares every corner of every
+item, button, and panel. Each flush corner rule ties on specificity with the boundary rule it
+overrides and follows it in the partial, which is why the collapsed button carries a flush selector
+of its own.
+
+A hovered button lifts to a `z-index` value of `2`, and a focused one lifts to a `z-index` value of
+`3`, so its ring paints over the borders the neighbouring items draw. The ring is the recorded
+quarter-rem shadow at a quarter of the palette blue, written as a mix over that token because a
+partial declares no literal color, and it answers to the `:focus` pseudo-class, as the release
+writes it. Under forced colors the same rule also writes an outline in the system highlight color
+through the `forced-ring` mixin, because forced colors paint no shadow ring.
+
+Each chevron is the release's own glyph, read out of the icon map in the `src/styles/_tokens.scss`
+file, because a data URI cannot read a custom property. The release's dark component rule gives the
+resting and the active chevron a second value, and the partial writes that rule with the same
+selector on the button's pseudo-element, where it outranks the light value the accordion declares.
+The dark theme scope declares neither chevron. Like the release's rule, the dark rule matches any
+button under a dark scope, so a light island nested inside a dark one keeps the dark chevrons.
+
+The button transition and the chevron transition carry Bootstrap's own `0.15s` and `0.2s` timings
+rather than the motion tokens the Button family reads, because this family ships the release's
+recorded surface. Each is written through the `transition` mixin, so the reduced-motion rule the
+release records beside it is emitted with it, and each duration resolves to a `0s` duration under
+that preference.
+
+These are the key's recorded departures.
+
+- **The block padding and the type size read Veneer's scales.** The button's and the body's block
+  padding read the `--vn-space-8` token and the button's type size reads the `--vn-size-3` token,
+  each of which already resolves to the release's `1rem` length, so the block padding rescales with
+  the `--vn-factor-density` factor. The inline padding and the chevron size keep the release's
+  `1.25rem` literal, because no space token resolves to that length, and the density factor leaves
+  them fixed.
+- **The focus ring mixes the palette blue.** The release writes the ring's color as a literal, and
+  the `--bs-accordion-btn-focus-box-shadow` property mixes the `--vn-palette-blue` token, which
+  carries that literal, at the same quarter strength.
+
+The Accordion region renders the release's own arrangement, the first item expanded above collapsed
+items, and a flush accordion whose middle item is expanded, so its first and last items rest
+collapsed. Each button announces its state through the `aria-expanded` attribute and names its panel
+through the `aria-controls` attribute.
+
+The `tests/src/styles/components/accordion.test.ts` proof reads each resolved treatment in the
+browser: the written selectors and the dark rule beside them, each published property beside the
+property it drives and retuned from the accordion itself, the expanded and collapsed paint with the
+inset line and the turned chevron, each state's own chevron, the boundary corners and the shared
+borders with a last item opened in markup, the flush group, the focus ring and the hover and focus
+lifts, the forced-colors outline, the dark chevrons against an empty theme scope, the light and dark
+modes, the density factor, and each transition at rest and under the staged preference.
+
